Abstract
Cancer-derived exosomes have recently emerged as potent candidates for diagnosis and prognosis of breast cancer. As an example, programmed death ligand-1 positive (PD-L1+) exosomes are found to be correlated with the progression and immunotherapy response of breast cancer, and therefore show great potential in liquid biopsy. Herein, we propose an electrochemical biosensing method for accurate identification of PD-L1+ exosomes by using DNA amplification-responsive metal-organic frameworks, PVP $@$ HRP@ZIF-8. Specially, PD-L1+ exosomes are captured by anti-CD63 functionalized magnetic beads and bound with anti-PD-L1-linked capture probe. Then, in situ hyperbranched rolling circle amplification, a typical DNA amplification reaction, is conducted using the surface-attached capture probes as primers, which lows environmental pH. As a result, disassembly of PVP@HRP $@$ ZIF-8 takes place, leading to the release of enzymes, which can arouse amplified electrochemical responses for the identification of target exosomes. Experimental results reveal that the biosensing method displays a linear range for PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes identification from $\mathsf{1}\times\mathsf{10}^{3}$ to $\boldsymbol{1\times}\boldsymbol{10}^{10}$ particles/mL and the detection limit reaches 334 particles/mL. What is more, by using the method, elevated level of circulating PD-L1+ exosomes is found in the undiluted serum samples from patients with breast cancer, particularly for metastatic breast cancer, revealing a positive correlation of the PD- ${\mathrm{.L1}}^{+}$ exosome level with the tumor staging and disease progression of breast cancer. Therefore, the biosensing method may be valuable for not only exosome identification but also providing reference information for diagnosis and real-time monitoring of breast cancer in the future.
Keywords: metal-organic framework, hyperbranched rolling circle amplification, breast cancer, programmed death ligand-1, electrochemical technique.
## 1. Introduction
Cell-secreted exosomes, a type of extracellular vesicles, are produced through the intracellular trafficking routine and widely distributed in body fluids after releasing from cell membrane, especially blood (Kalluri et al., 2020). The nano-sized vesicles contain typical phospholipid bilayer structures and carry abundant biological molecules derived from the original cells (Jeppesen et al., 2019). Normally, cell-secreted exosomes engage in multiple physiological processes relating to cellular communications, and differentiation and development of organisms (Mathieu et al., 2019; Thery et al., 2020). Besides, exosomes are found in several pathological processes. For example, emerging evidences reveal that cancer-derived exosomes are associated with tumorigenesis, invasion, metastasis and drug resistance in tumors (Wortzel et al., 2019; Yu et al., 2015). Cancer-derived exosomes are also gaining increasing attention in liquid biopsy, and represent the superiority in absolute quantity over circulating tumor cells that are extremely rare even at the late stage of cancers and provide more information than cell-free nucleic acids (Fitts et al., 2019; Poudineh et al., 2018). Moreover, surface-expressed proteins on exosomes have close relationship with the original tumors and may reflect abundant biological information for precious management of the tumors. For instance, programmed death ligand-1 (PD-L1), an important immune checkpoint molecule, is found to be expressed on exosomes derived from breast cancer, the most common malignant tumor among women worldwide (Chen et al., 2018a; Yang et al., 2018). The level of PD-L1 positive (PD-L1+) exosomes is potentially a prognostic prediction marker of anti-PD-1 immunotherapy, which may be critical for cancer management (Daassi et al., 2020; Huang et al., 2020; Tang et al., 2020). Whereas, determination of PD-L1+ exosome level is still challenging in breast cancer, mostly due to the limited biosensing method for accurate identification of PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes (Boriachek et al., 2018;  Cheng et al., 2019).
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are currently the most attractive porous nanomaterials for biosensing application (Fang et al., 2018). The nanoscaled inorganic-organic hybrids exhibit several distinct advantages over other existing nanomaterials, including adjustable structure and surface functionalization for diverse functions, large pore volumes for high loading capabilities, and biodegradable and biocompatible nanoformula for in vivo transportation. Encouraged by biomineralization, multiple biological molecules (e.g. biomacromolecules and cells) are encapsulated into MOFs, thus MOFs are popular for intracellular delivery of nucleic acids and proteins (Cheng et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2019). More interestingly, the disassembly of MOFs can be modulated by different endogenous or exogenous stimulus, such as pH, ions, temperature, pressure, redox and magnetic field (Chen et al., 2018b; Yang et al., 2019; Zhong et al., 2019; Zhou et al., 2018). For instance, zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8), a typical kind of MOFs, is known for inherent pH-responsive ability, which displays improved release efficiency at an acidic environment rather than the neutral or alkaline condition. Protein-encapsulating ZIF-8 prepared by biomineralization-facilitated method is able to realize pH-responsive release of native active proteins in the live cells via endo-lysosomes (Chen et al., 2018b). At the meanwhile, DNA polymerase that catalyzes the incorporation of nucleotides into DNA chain is also demonstrated to promote steady release of protons from $3^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl group of extensive DNA chain during nucleic acid amplification, thereby dynamically changing environmental pH under a low-buffered condition (Hua et al., 2019; Mao et al., 2019; Toumazou et al., 2013). Combining DNA amplification-promoted pH changes and pH-responsive protein-encapsulating ZIF-8, we herein reported a biosensing method for accurate identification of PD-L1+ exosomes in breast cancer by virtue of electrochemical technique. To best of our knowledge, it is the first time to quantify PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes in the blood samples of breast cancer patients, which reveals a potential correlation between the PD-L1+ exosome level and the advanced stage and poor prognosis of breast cancer.
Scheme 1 illustrates the principle of the method. pH-responsive protein-encapsulating ZIF-8 (PVP@HRP $@$ ZIF-8) were prepared by encapsulation of horseradish peroxidase (HRP) into ZIF-8 and subsequent surface coating by polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), which were stable at weak alkaline pH and disassembled at acidic pH. PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes derived from breast cancer were first accumulated on the surface of anti-CD63 functionalized magnetic beads (anti- $\mathrm{CD}63@\mathbf{MBs}$ ) due to the selective recognition of the established exosomal biomarker, CD63, and also specially bound with anti-PD-L1-linked DNA strand, namely capture probe, utilizing the antibody part of capture probe. Subsequently, DNA part within capture probe worked as a primer to initiate a typical DNA amplification reaction, hyperbranched rolling circle amplification (HRCA). Environmental pH was gradually decreased during the HRCA process because hydrogen ions was sustainably released along with the continuous addition of nucleotides, which then mediated the disassembly of PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8 at a mildly acidic environment. As a result, HRP was released and used to arouse amplified electrochemical responses, and thus realized identification of breast cancer-derived PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes.
## 2. Material and methods
## 2.1. Materials
HRP, 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM), zinc nitrate hexahydrate, sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) and hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ were purchased from Sangon Biotech Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Phi29 DNA polymerase and dNTPs were obtained from New England Biolabs (Beijing, China). PVP, $o$ -phenylenediamine (OPD), sulfosuccinimidyl-4-(N-maleimidomethyl)cyclohexane-1-carboxylate (sulfo-SMCC), tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP), N-hydroxysuccinimide (NHS), and N-(3-Dimethylaminopropyl)-N'-ethlcarbodiimide hydrochloride (EDC) were ordered from Sigma-Aldrich (Shanghai, China). MDA-MB-231, MCF-7, and L02 cell lines were purchased from the Institute of Biochemistry and Cell Biology of Chinese Academy of Science (Shanghai, China). Dulbecco's modified Eagle medium (DMEM), RPMI-1640 medium, and fetal bovine serum (FBS) were obtained from Gibco Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China). Carboxylated MBs and $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -dioctadecyloxacarbocyanine perchlorate (Dio) were purchased from Invitrogen (Shanghai, China). Anti-CD63 antibody and anti-PD-L1 antibody were purchased from Abcam (Shanghai, China). All oligonucleotides used in this work were synthesized by Sangon Biotech Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China), and their sequences were listed in Table S1.
## 2.2. Synthesis of PVP@HRP@ZIF-8
PVP@HRP@ZIF-8 was synthesized according to previous report with some modifications (Chen et al., 2018b). First, HRP $\mathrm{(0.7~mg)}$ ) was diluted in an aqueous solution of 2-MIM (3.15 mM, 0.9 mL) and stirred at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . Then, a solution of zinc nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{0.045~mM,0.1~mL}$ ) was added into the above mixture and stirred at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for another $10\mathrm{min}$ to prepare HRP $@$ ZIF-8. The prepared HRP $@$ ZIF-8 was centrifuged at $3500\mathrm{rpm}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ , and washed twice with ethanol. Then, HRP $@$ ZIF-8 was dissolved in SDS $5\%$ , w/w) aqueous solution at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove free proteins, and centrifuged at $14000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ and washed three times with deionized water to remove excessive SDS. Finally, HRP $@$ ZIF-8 was mixed with PVP (1 mL, $3\%$ , w/w) and stirred for 30 min to obtain
PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8, which was stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for future use. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) characterization of $\mathrm{PVP}@\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ was performed with a HT7700 electron microscope (Hitachi, Japan) at $200~\mathrm{kV}.$ . Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was performed on a VERTEX70 FTIR spectrometer (Braker, Germany). Dynamic light scattering (DLS) characterization was performed on a Zetasizer Nano (Malvern, UK).
## 2.3. Preparation of immune-MBs and capture probes
For the preparation of immune-MBs (anti-CD63 $@$ MB and anti-PD-L1 $@$ MB), carboxyl MBs $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $10\mathrm{\mg/mL}$ ) were washed three times by PBS, and then activated using EDC/NHS $(500~\upmu\mathrm{L},0.22~\mathrm{M})$ at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . After washing by PBS, the activated MBs were further reacted with antibodies (anti-CD63 or anti-PD-L1) $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L},~20~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , and the obtained immune-MBs were magnetically separated from the solution and re-dispersed in PBS for further use. For the preparation of capture probes, anti-PD-L1 $(10\upmu\mathrm{L},2.4\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and sulfo-SMCC ( $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $10\ \mathrm{mM}$ ) were mixed in PBS ${(80~\upmu\mathrm{L})}$ at $30~\bigsqcup$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . At the meantime, P1 ( $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{M}})$ and TCEP ( $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $200~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ were also mixed in PBS $(80~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ at $30~\square$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Then, the activated antibodies were mixed with the activated P1 at room temperature and incubated for $2\mathrm{h}$ . Afterwards, the mixture was transferred to $30~\mathrm{KD}$ ultrafiltration centrifuge tube and centrifuged at $14000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and repeated for 3 times to collect the capture probes.
## 2.4. Cell culture and exosome isolation
MDA-MB-231 and L02 cells were grown in RPMI-1640 medium supplemented with $10\%$ FBS and MCF-7 cells were grown in DMEM with $10\%$ FBS. All cells were cultured at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $80\%$ relative humidity. Cell-derived exosomes were isolated from the supernatant of culture medium of the three cell lines according to previous work with some modifications (Tian et al., 2020). Specially, cells were separated from the culture medium when grown to $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ confluence and washed twice with PBS. After incubation for $24{\cdot}48\mathrm{~h~}$ in serum-free medium, the culture medium was collected and centrifuged at $200\times\mathrm{g}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ and $3000\times\mathrm{g}$ for $15~\mathrm{min}$ at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in sequence to remove intact cells and cellular debris. Thereafter, the supernatant was transferred to $12000\times\mathrm{g}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After being filtered with a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ filter, the supernatant was further purified with a $100~\mathrm{KD}$ ultrafiltration centrifuge tube at $4000\times\mathrm{g}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the upper filtrate containing exosomes was dispersed in $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ centrifuge tube, and stored at $-80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before further use. For expression analysis of CD63 and PD-L1 on exosome surface, immune-MBs (anti- $\mathbf{CD63@MB}$ and anti-PD-L1 $@$ MB, respectively) were used to capture exosomes. After magnetic separation, the captured exosomes were stained by Dio $(30\upmu\mathrm{M})$ for 30 min, and then brought to analysis on a MoFlo XDP flow cytometer (Beckman Coulter).
## 2.5. Characterization of HRCA
HRCA was conducted in a ${60}\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ mixture solution, including P1 $(500\ \mathrm{nM})$ ), P2 (1 $\upmu\mathrm{M})$ ), Template $(500~\mathrm{nM})$ , dNTPs ( $\mathrm{i0~mM})$ and phi29 DNA polymerase $(12{\mathrm{~U}})$ . The initial $\mathsf{p H}$ was adjusted with KOH $20~\mathrm{mM})$ to 8.0, and the reaction was performed at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ , which was then terminated at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 20 min. Agarose gel electrophoresis (AGE) and $\mathrm{\pH}$ test were used for the characterization of HRCA. For AGE, DNA samples $10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were mixed with $6\times$ loading buffer $(2~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ containing SYBR Green I and separated in $1\times\mathrm{TAE}$ at $80~\mathrm{V}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . After being turned into $120{\mathrm{V}}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , gel imaging was performed using a Gel Doc XR Imaging system. For pH test, DNA samples $(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were dropped on the $\mathsf{p H}$ test papers, and the colors were photographed 90 s later.
## 2.6. Electrochemical identification of PD- $.L I^{+}$ exosomes
For identification of ${\mathrm{PD-L1}}^{+}$ exosomes, anti-CD63 $@$ MB ( $100~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ and capture probes $(80~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were first mixed with different concentrations of PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes at room temperature for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, the MBs were washed twice by PBS, and further mixed with HRCA reaction solution $(60~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ containing P2 $(1~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , Template (500 nM), dNTPs ( $\mathrm{i0~mM)}$ and phi29 DNA polymerase $|12\mathrm{~U})$ . HRCA was conducted at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ and then terminated at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Thereafter, $\mathrm{PVP}@\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ $(6.5~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $7.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , 40 mM) and OPD $7.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $40~\mathrm{mM}$ ) were added to the HRCA reaction mixture and incubated for $45~\mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the mixture was diluted to $2~\mathrm{mL}$ with PBS and used for electrochemical measurements. Electrochemical measurements were performed on CHI 660C workstation (Shanghai, China) with the use of a three-electrode system, including a platinum wire as the auxiliary electrode, a saturated calomel electrode as the reference electrode, and a glassy carbon electrode as the working electrode. Electrochemical responses were recorded with square wave voltammetry (SWV) which was performed from $-1.1\mathrm{V}$ to $-0.2\mathrm{V}.$ .
## 2.7. Preparation of clinical samples
Clinical experiments were approved by the Scientific Ethical Committee of Fudan University Shanghai Cancer Center and performed in accordance with the ethical standards. Blood samples were collected from healthy volunteers and breast cancer patients at Minhang Branch of Fudan University Shanghai Cancer Center (Shanghai, China) after obtaining informed consent. Each blood sample was left at room temperature for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , and then the supernatant was taken. Afterward, the sample was centrifuged at $1550\times\mathrm{g}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ to pellet all cells, and then centrifuged at $1200\times\mathrm{g}$ for $20\ \mathrm{min}$ to remove residual cellular debris. Finally, the supernatant was gently collected and stored at $-80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before use.
## 3. Results and Discussion
