Fig. 1A shows schematic illustration of the preparation of PVP@HRP@ZIF-8. To this end, HRP@ZIF-8 was first synthesized through a biomimetic mineralization method, and PVP coating was further applied to control pore penetration and increase long-term stability and dispersibility of the nanocomposites (Chen et al., 2018b). Fig. S1 displays the characterization of PVP $@$ HRP@ZIF-8. TEM was used to demonstrate their nanoscale monodisperse morphology (Fig. S1A) and DLS analysis showed an average size of ${\sim}330~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S1B), which were in line with the previous report (Chen et al., 2018b). FTIR was also performed. As shown in Fig. S1C, characteristic absorption bands at $1660~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1560~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were observed for HRP@ZIF-8 and PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8, respectively, attributing to amide I and II bands from HRP, which demonstrated the encapsulation of the enzymes.
As one of the core elements of this work, pH-responsive ability of PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8 was fully investigated. Fig. 1B shows the results of SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) analysis. Emerged protein bands indicated that encapsulated HRP could be preferably released from PVP@HRP $@$ ZIF-8 at $\mathrm{pH}~6.5$ but rarely released at $\mathrm{\pH\8.0}$ . Fig. S2A shows the UV-vis spectrum for OPD oxidation by HRP released from PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8. The maximum absorption peak at $425~\mathrm{nm}$ demonstrated the well maintenance of catalytic ability of released HRP. It can also be seen from Fig. S2B that the absorption peak increased with the decrease of pH values from 8.0 to 6.5 and the solution color correspondingly changed from colorless to yellow, which suggested gradually enhanced release of HRP, confirming the pH-responsive ability of PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8. Fig. 1C further compares the pH-responsive abilities of $\mathrm{PVP}@\mathrm{HRP}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ and HRP $@$ ZIF-8 by using UV-vis spectra. Comparably high absorption at the wavelength of $425~\mathrm{nm}$ was observed at $\mathrm{pH}~6.5$ for both PVP $@$ HRP@ZIF-8 and HRP $@$ ZIF-8, attributing to HRP-catalyzed oxidation of OPD with the addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ after the MOFs disassembly. In contrast, much lower absorption was observed for
PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8 than HRP $@$ ZIF-8 at $\mathrm{pH}8.0$ , suggesting the improved stability for PVP coating under the alkaline condition. Overall, SDS-PAGE and UV-vis spectra demonstrated the successful preparation and pH-responsive ability of PVP@HRP $@$ ZIF-8, and the encapsulated HRP well maintained catalytic ability toward OPD oxidation.
3.2. Isolation and characterization of breast cancer-derived PD- $.L I^{+}$ exosomes line MDA-MB-231: (A) TEM image and (B) size distribution analysis using NTA. (C) to (F) represent flow cytometry analysis of surface expression of CD63 using anti- $.\mathrm{CD}63@$ MB and PD-L1 using anti-PD-L1 $@$ MB..
Exosomes derived from triple-negative breast cancer line MDA-MB-231 were used as model breast cancer-derived PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes in this work. TEM was first performed to characterize the exosomes, conforming a typical structure of lipid bilayer membrane (Fig. 2A). The nanoparticle tracking analysis (NTA) revealed the size of ${\sim}30{\cdot}170\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2B). Expression of CD63 and PD-L1 on the surface of MDA-MB-231-derived exosomes were investigated by using flow cytometry (Fig. 2C to 2E). For this purpose, anti-CD63 and anti-PD-L1 were used to prepare immune-MBs (anti-CD63 $@$ MB and anti-PD-L1 $@$ MB) respectively. After staining MB-captured MDA-MB-231-derived exosomes by Dio, fluorescence shifts from the background confirmed the existence of surface-expressed CD63 and PD-L1.
Furthermore, simultaneous recognition of CD63 and PD-L1 on the PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes was revealed by using fluorescence microscopy, in which anti- $\mathbf{CD63@MB}$ and Cy3-labeled capture probe that consisted of anti-PD-L1 and Cy3-labeled P1 were used for target binding. The fluorescence image first demonstrated the capture of exosomes on anti- $\mathbf{CD63}@$ MB by Dio staining. A bright green fluorescence was observed with the addition of exosomes (Fig. 3A) but nearly no fluorescence was observed without exosomes (Fig. 3B), suggesting the capture of exosomes through CD63-based immune-affinity interaction. Moreover, intense yellow fluorescence of Cy3 was observed on MB-captured exosomes after adding Cy3-labeled capture probe (Fig. 3C), but no fluorescence signaling was observed when using Cy3-labeled P1 instead of Cy3-labeled capture probe (Fig. 3D). The results suggested the formation of sandwich-like structure at MB surface, which strongly relied on the recognition of exosomal PD-L1 and anti-PD-L1 within capture probe.
