3.3. Electrochemical identification of PD-L1+ exosomes
HRCA was essential to induce pH changes of the reaction microenvironment under a low-buffered condition, so the process of HRCA was examined by AGE and pH test papers. Fig. S3A shows the schematic illustration of the reaction process and accompanying release of protons involved in HRCA. Specially, P1 triggered polymerase-catalyzed DNA amplification with the use of a circular template (Template); a reverse primer, P2, was then recruited to favor primer extension and strand displacement simultaneously, together constituting HRCA. Fig. S3B shows the AGE image with the input of different DNA strands with or without DNA polymerase. Lane 1 displayed the amplification band of HRCA, but no amplification band was observed in the absence of P1 in Lane 2, suggesting the critical role of P1 in initiation of HRCA. Lane 3 and 4 showed no bands for the amplification reaction without the input of DNA polymerase even after the addition of P1, proving the necessity of DNA polymerase in HRCA. Fig. S3C shows the corresponding pH changes using $\mathrm{\pH}$ test paper. The solution pH remained stable at ${\sim}8.0$ in the absence of P1 or/and DNA polymerase (Lane 1-3) while decreased to ${\sim}6.5$ after HRCA (Lane 4). The results were in agreement of DNA amplification-promoted proton release under low-buffered condition. Fig. S3D further shows a high absorbance peak of OPD oxidation after HRCA but quite low absorbance without HRCA, which indicated HRCA-mediated release of HRP from PVP@HRP@ZIF-8, confirming the HRCA-responsive property of the nanocomposites.
(c) that with PD-L1+ exosomes but without HRCA. (B) Peak currents of electrochemical responses obtained upon analyzing ${10}^{10}$ particles/mL exosomes isolated from MDA-MB-231, MCF-7 and L02 cells, respectively. (C) Electrochemical responses with different concentrations of PD- ${\mathrm{.L}}1^{+}$ exosomes (From a to i: $),10^{3},10^{4}$ , $10^{5},\ 10^{6},\ 10^{7},\ 10^{8},\ 10^{9}$ , and ${10}^{10}$ particles/mL). (D) The relationship between peak current and exosome concentration and the linear relationship between peak current and logarithmic value of exosome concentration in the range from ${{10}^{3}}$ to ${10}^{10}$ particles/mL (inset).
Electrochemical techniques were then applied to validate the feasibility of the method. Fig. 4A shows the electrochemical responses with or without the addition of PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes. High electrochemical response was observed with the addition of PD-L1+ exosomes after HRCA-mediated release of HRP and subsequent OPD oxidation (curve a), but quite low electrochemical responses were observed without PD- ${\mathrm{.L1}}^{+}$ exosomes (curve b) or that with PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes but without the occurrence of HRCA (curve c). The results were as expected. Exosomes were enriched on anti-CD63@MB, and also bound with the capture probe through anti-PD-L1 to trigger HRCA. Sustainably released protons promoted the disassembly of PVP $@$ HRP $@$ ZIF-8, and then catalyzed the oxidation of OPD by the released HRP for amplified electrochemical response. Conversely, the lack of exosomes inhibited HRCA for the missing surface-attached capture probe, identical to that with PD-L1+ exosomes but without DNA polymerases. Fig. 4B shows the electrochemical responses with the addition of same amounts of exosomes derived from different cell lines respectively. High electrochemical response was observed for exosomes derived from MDA-MB-231, but lower electrochemical response was observed for MCF-7-derived exosomes and quite low electrochemical response approaching background signaling was observed for L02-derived exosomes. The results revealed a distinct PD-L1+ exosome level in the three cell lines: PD-L1 was highly expressed in MDA-MB-231-derived exosomes, but expressed in MCF-7-derived exosomes at a low level and negatively expressed in L02-derived exosomes. This was in agreement with the previous report (Yang et al., 2018), demonstrating the feasibility of the method for accurate identification of PD-L1 exosomes.
Afterward, several optimization experiments were performed. Fig. S4 shows the peak currents with different reaction time for the capture of exosomes and binding with capture probe, suggesting the optimal time of 60 min. Fig. S5 shows the peak currents with different reaction time for HRCA, suggesting the optimal time of 120 min. Fig. 4C shows the electrochemical responses with different concentrations of PD- ${\mathrm{.L}}1^{+}$ exosomes under the optimal conditions. Obviously, peak currents increased with the enhancement of exosome concentrations. This is reasonable, because elevated amounts of surface-enriched PD- ${\mathrm{.L}}1^{+}$ exosomes provided increased binding sites for the loading of capture probes, and thus enhanced the reaction efficiency of HRCA to promote the disassembly of PVP@HRP@ZIF-8 and the subsequent oxidation of OPD. Fig. 4D shows the correlation of peak currents and concentrations of exosomes, and demonstrated a linear relationship between the peak current and the logarithmic value of ${\mathrm{PD-L1}}^{+}$ exosome concentration in the range from ${{10}^{3}}$ to $10^{10}$ particles/mL. The linear equation was $I\left(\upmu\mathrm{A}\right)=0.181\times\mathrm{Log}C_{e x o s e}$ omes (particles/mL) $^+$
0.037, $R^{2}=0.992$ , and the limit of detection (LOD) was estimated to be 334 particles/mL at the signal-to-noise ratio of 3. Compared to prior studies, the method demonstrated an improved sensitivity with wider linear range and lower LOD (Table S2). Electrochemical measurements for each concentration were repeated at least three times with the average of RSDs as $3.85\%$ for satisfactory reproducibility. In addition, the anti-disturbance capability of the method was evaluated by using diluted FBS as complex sample. As shown in Fig. S6, the peak currents obtained upon analyzing PD-L1+ exosomes in PBS buffer and diluted FBS were comparable, confirming high selectivity and recovery of the method in complex environment.
3.4. Identification of $P D\ –L I^{+}$ exosomes in breast cancer clinical samples
Fig. 5 (A) Peak currents of electrochemical responses for clinical serum samples. (B) Scatter plots of the peak currents of serum samples from healthy controls, non-metastatic breast cancer patients and metastatic breast cancer patients measured by using the method.
We also examined the application of the method in monitoring the personalized level of PD-L1+ exosomes in breast cancer clinical samples. To this end, undiluted serum from healthy persons $(n~=~6)$ ), breast cancer patients with non-metastatic diseases $(n=8)$ ) and metastatic diseases $(n=7$ ) were collected as clinical samples. The information of all the subjects was listed in Table S3. Fig. 5A shows the obtained peak currents for serum samples. Low peak currents were observed for the serum of the healthy persons due to the lack of PD- ${\mathrm{.L}}1^{+}$ exosomes, and increased peak currents were observed for the serum samples from the breast cancer patients, suggesting the presence of PD-L1+ exosomes. In the meantime, peak currents for breast cancer patients with metastatic diseases were higher than those with non-metastatic diseases, which was consistence with the advanced disease progression and worse prognosis in breast tumors. Fig. 5B further shows the scatter plots of peak currents for serum samples. The low average value of $0.5040~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ was calculated for controls, and the average of $0.9011~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ and $1.525~\upmu\mathrm{A}$ were accounted for patients with non-metastatic and metastatic diseases, respectively. The average peak currents for breast cancer patients were significantly higher than that for healthy controls, and the average peak currents for metastatic patients was also significantly higher than that for non-metastatic patients. It was worth noting that normal cell-derived exosomes in the serum samples that were also CD63-positive had little impact on the electrochemical measurements, reconfirming the accurate identification of PD-L1+ exosomes by using the method. The electrochemical identification of PD-L1+ exosomes in clinical samples suggested that PD-L1+ exosomes might be a potential biomarker to predict the occurrence of breast cancer at an early stage, and help to monitor the disease progression during the treatment.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we proposed an electrochemical biosensing method for identification of PD-L1+ exosomes using HRCA-mediated disassembly of PVP $@$ HRP@ZIF-8. The prepared PVP@HRP@ZIF-8 were stable at alkaline environment to reduce background signaling. The HRCA-induced changes of environmental $\mathsf{p H}$ facilitated the disassembly of PVP@HRP@ZIF-8, which was known as the first signal amplification, and HRP-catalyzed oxidation of OPD was regarded as the second signal amplification. The multiple signal amplification contributed to high sensitivity of the method with a wider linear range and lower LOD. At the meanwhile, the selective and high-affinity binding of surface biomarkers and the corresponding antibodies ensured the high specificity of the method. Moreover, the method could be used for identification of PD- ${\cdot}\mathrm{L}1^{+}$ exosomes in clinical samples and found that circulating PD-L1+ exosomes might be a valuable marker for diagnosis and progression monitoring of breast cancer. In view of these features, we anticipate that the method may have a cheerful application prospect in exosome identification and breast cancer research.
